<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/1st-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Early Muslim conquests</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Islamic conquest, Arab conquests</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The early Muslim conquests were the rapid expansion of the Arab Muslim state in the 7th century, following the death of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>. Arab armies overran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and expansion continued under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>. The conquests also disrupted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> trade, contributing to the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests.jpg" alt="Early Muslim conquests" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DieBuche · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_expansion_of_Caliphate.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rise of Islam and the <b>Arab conquests</b> of Arabia and Egypt in the seventh century completed Aksum's commercial isolation, ending the trade-based prosperity that had sustained the kingdom for some five centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The theme system, developed during the seventh and eighth centuries in response to the <b>Arab conquests</b>, reorganised the remaining empire into military-administrative districts whose soldiers were supported by land grants in return for service, creating a largely self-sustaining provincial defence that reduced dependence on costly mercenaries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…I, of the traditional harvest-share tax, which required annual assessment and invited official corruption, with a fixed land tax based on surveys of cultivated area, crop type and irrigation, a reform that produced more stable revenues and survived the <b>Islamic conquest</b> into the fiscal systems of the caliphate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Arab conquests</b> ended Byzantine rule in the Levant, Egypt and North Africa and destroyed the Sasanian Empire, beginning a transformation of these lands into predominantly Arabic-speaking and ultimately Muslim societies, a change that proved irreversible across most of the conquered territory.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Islam spread first with the <b>Arab conquests</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In Sicily and Malta, Arabic was spoken after the <b>Arab conquests</b>, and Maltese, written in Latin letters, descends from it.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
